<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yoshida-Kōriyama Castle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yoshida-Kōriyama Castle was a mountain castle in Aki province, now in Akitakata in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima-prefecture">Hiroshima Prefecture</a>, the home base of the Mōri clan. Mōri Motonari successfully defended it against a large Amago army in 1540–1541, a victory that began his rise to power over western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It was abandoned after his grandson Terumoto moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> Castle in 1591, and its extensive earthworks survive as a national historic site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yoshida-koriyama-castle.jpg" alt="Yoshida-Kōriyama Castle" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TT mk2 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yoshida-kouriyama_Castle_Honmaru.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The castle stands in the Yoshida basin in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima-prefecture">Hiroshima Prefecture</a>, ringed by the Chugoku Mountains and drained by two rivers, the Gonokawa flowing north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> and the Otagawa flowing south to the Seto Inland Sea. It was also called Aki-Kōriyama Castle after the old province. The Mōri clan, descended from Ōe no Hiromoto, a leading retainer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> shogunate, first built it as a small fort in the 14th century. Hiromoto's fourth son, Mōri Suemitsu, held a shōen estate in Aki Province, and his descendants became leaders of an alliance of local lords in eastern Aki and western Bingo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Aki was a contested border between the Ouchi clan of Suo Province and the Yamana, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Onin War</a> the Izumo-based Amago rose to challenge the Ouchi; the Mōri changed sides between them several times to survive. Under Mōri Motonari, who allied with the Ouchi, the castle was greatly enlarged into the clan's main stronghold, and the Amago siege of September 1540 ended in their defeat, with Ouchi help, in January 1541. Rebuilt to cover most of the mountain, it became an advanced mountaintop castle, or yamashiro, with inner and outer stone walls and a stone-walled fort.</p><p>Motonari gave the castle to his eldest son, Mōri Takamoto, and after the Amago were decisively defeated in 1566 he had it strengthened further. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mōri Terumoto</a> began Hiroshima Castle as his new seat in 1589. After the Mōri sided with the western alliance at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600, they lost Aki Province, and the castle was largely demolished early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> under the Tokugawa policy of one castle to a domain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The ruins have been a National Historic Site since 1940 and are now a historical park, with low stone walls, moats and about 130 remains spread over the mountain. Artefacts excavated there are shown at the nearby Akitakata City Historical Museum, and in 2006 the Japan Castle Foundation listed the castle among Japan's Top 100 Castles.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yoshida-Kōriyama Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mori Motonari, a minor lord of Aki based at <b>Yoshida-Koriyama</b> castle, built up power between the Ouchi and Amago.</blockquote><a href="/state/mori-clan-domain">Mori clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
